    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, i thought salt and cortisol were different? and what do you mean about melanin? i was thinking more about the vagus nerve or the parasympathetic nervous system part of it. how does the breathing actually signal the brain to calm down?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Cortisol and melanin are distinct. Melanin isn't directly related to the brain signaling during box breathing. \n\nWhen you breathe in a 4-4-4-4 pattern, the increase in oxygen-rich blood flow stimulates the vagus nerve, which is part of the parasympathetic nervous system. The vagus nerve helps regulate heart rate, relaxation, and calmness. The downstream effect is an activation of the parasympathetic nervous system, promoting relaxation and reducing stress hormones like cortisol, ultimately calming the nervous system.",
      "source": "target",
