    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's basically like a manual override for the body. is there any specific reason why the 'holds' are so important? like does the pause in breathing trigger something specific in the brain compared to just slow breathing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The holds in box breathing are crucial because they selectively stimulate the vagus nerve's vagal tone. The pause in breathing triggers the brain's baroreceptors to signal the parasympathetic nervous system to activate. This specific type of pause, called the expiratory pause, is unique to box breathing and isn't achieved through just slow breathing. The consecutive stabilization of pressure (pressure increase then decrease) during the hold triggers a specific response in the brain.",
      "source": "target",
